## Proof

**The statement is TRUE.**

**Claim.** If $(\mathcal{F}_0, \Vert\cdot\Vert_\infty)$ is a metric space of continuous functions that is dense in $\mathcal{F}$ (with respect to the $\Vert\cdot\Vert_\infty$ metric), then for every $f \in \mathcal{F}$ there exists a sequence $(f_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ in $\mathcal{F}_0$ that converges uniformly to $f$.

**Proof.**

Recall that density of $\mathcal{F}_0$ in $\mathcal{F}$ with respect to the metric $d(g,h)=\Vert g-h\Vert_\infty$ means:

$$\forall\, f\in\mathcal{F},\ \forall\, \varepsilon>0,\ \exists\, g\in\mathcal{F}_0 \text{ such that } \Vert f-g\Vert_\infty < \varepsilon. \tag{$\ast$}$$

We construct the desired sequence as follows. For each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, apply $(\ast)$ with $\varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{n}$ to obtain an element $f_n \in \mathcal{F}_0$ satisfying

$$\Vert f - f_n\Vert_\infty < \frac{1}{n}.$$

This produces a sequence $(f_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ in $\mathcal{F}_0$.

We now verify uniform convergence. By definition, $f_n \to f$ uniformly means $\Vert f - f_n\Vert_\infty \to 0$ as $n\to\infty$. For any $\varepsilon > 0$, choose $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $N > 1/\varepsilon$. Then for all $n \geq N$,

$$\Vert f - f_n\Vert_\infty < \frac{1}{n} \leq \frac{1}{N} < \varepsilon.$$

Hence $\Vert f - f_n\Vert_\infty \to 0$, i.e., $f_n \to f$ uniformly. $\blacksquare$

**Remark on why uniform convergence is automatic here.** The key point is that the metric on $\mathcal{F}_0$ is *specifically* the supremum norm $\Vert\cdot\Vert_\infty$. Convergence in the supremum norm *is exactly* uniform convergence. Therefore, density in this metric is *equivalent* to the statement that every $f\in\mathcal{F}$ is a uniform limit of a sequence from $\mathcal{F}_0$. There is no gap between "approximation in the metric" and "uniform approximation" — they are the same notion. The demand for uniform convergence is satisfied by the very definition of density with respect to $\Vert\cdot\Vert_\infty$.

